<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Husayn ibn Ahmad al-Habili</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">20th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sharif Husayn of Beihan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Husayn ibn Ahmad al-Habili was the sharif of Beihan, a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> on the frontier between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. One of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s firmest allies in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, he resisted Yemeni claims to the border in the 1950s and was a founding figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-arab-emirates-of-the-south">Federation of Arab Emirates of the South</a> in 1959. He went into exile when the British withdrew in 1967.</p></div>
